--
-- What users have been told. ReadAt separates seen from unseen, which is the
-- question the table exists to answer; several rows are deliberately unread.
-- Not every notification is about a task -- the draft assumed they all were.
--
-- "ActorUUID" is who caused it, and it is NULL wherever nobody did: a task
-- falls overdue on its own, a level is reached by the person being told, and
-- an update ships. The bell draws the actor as the face on the row, so the
-- dataset needs both kinds or half of that code is never seen.
--
-- A message on a row that has an actor is read **after** the actor's name --
-- "Jane Doe assigned you Build the API." -- so it is written as a phrase that
-- follows one. A message on a row with no actor is a whole sentence.
--
-- "CreatedAt" is written here, relative to the moment of the seed run, rather
-- than left to default. The bell prints how long ago each one arrived, so a
-- dataset where everything was created at once has nothing to show: these are
-- minutes, hours, days and weeks old on a freshly seeded database. They age
-- from there, which is what a notification does.
--
-- Which means this file has a wrinkle the others do not: "CreatedAt" is left
-- out of the DO UPDATE, because the triggers own the audit columns, so
-- re-running `make db-seed` over rows that already exist refreshes their
-- message and leaves their age where it was. After editing the ages here, use
-- `make db-reseed` -- or delete the seeded rows from this one table -- or the
-- list will sort by the old timestamps and not by what is written below.
--

